{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_014N64dSqEJ69pErs9bvubGP","type":"tool_result","content":"---\nrole: lead\nstatus: done\nattempt: 1\nnext: [triage:engineer]\nworker: engineer\n---\nThe fix is to make a worker change to any .gitattributes file go to a human, either in the committer's code-path check or as a built-in merge hold, and to name that rule in docs/proposal.md. The change is confined to Go code and the proposal document, so the engineer plans it and the engineer worker implements it.\n\n## Routing\n- triage:engineer: decide where the hold lives (internal/committer/codepath.go or the merger's hold logic), write the steps, and plan tests plus the docs/proposal.md wording that names the rule.\n- worker engineer: Go change with unit tests and the proposal doc update.\n\n## Open issues\n- Agent reviewers already get a raw text diff that ignores attributes (internal/gitx/review.go, internal/reviewer/doc.go). The remaining gap is the collapsed diff that humans see on GitHub, so the plan should cover that and need not rebuild the reviewer diff.\n- One option in the issue is to add .gitattributes to merge.protected_paths in .ard/config.yaml. Agents may not edit that file, so if maintainers want that option instead of a code-level hold, a person has to make the config change.\npackage committer\n\nimport (\n\t\"path\"\n\t\"strings\"\n\t\"unicode\"\n)\n\n// CheckCodePath reports why a worker may not commit p for issue, or nil. It is\n// the one copy of the rule: the worker's diff guard (#33) and CommitCode both\n// call it. Paths must already be canonical; they are rejected, not normalised.\n//\n// Refused: a .git segment, .gitmodules, everything under .ard/ (role, config\n// and issue files; the issue's own go through close-out, not through worker\n// code), everything under .ard-input/ (loop inputs), everything under the\n// root .github/ (not only workflows), and secret-shaped file names. Only the\n// repository-root .github/ is reserved; docs/.github/x is ordinary. The\n// reserved first segments match case-insensitively because a case-insensitive\n// filesystem would fold them.\n//\n// issue is accepted for the message and future use; it does not change the\n// outcome today, because every .ard/ path is refused.\nfunc CheckCodePath(issue int, p string) error {\n\t_ = issue\n\tif p == \"\" {\n\t\treturn reason(\"empty path\")\n\t}\n\tfor _, r := range p {\n\t\tif r == '\\\\' || r < 0x20 || r == 0x7f || r == 0xFFFD || unicode.Is(unicode.Cf, r) {\n\t\t\treturn reason(\"backslash, control or invisible character\")\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\tif path.Clean(p) != p || strings.HasPrefix(p, \"/\") {\n\t\treturn reason(\"not canonical\")\n\t}\n\tsegs := strings.Split(p, \"/\")\n\tfor _, s := range segs {\n\t\tif s == \".\" || s == \"..\" {\n\t\t\treturn reason(\"not canonical\")\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\tfor _, seg := range segs {\n\t\tif strings.EqualFold(seg, \".git\") {\n\t\t\treturn reason(\"contains .git\")\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\tif len(segs) > 1 {\n\t\tswitch strings.ToLower(segs[0]) {\n\t\tcase \".ard\":\n\t\t\treturn reason(\"under .ard/ (role, config and issue files are not worker code)\")\n\t\tcase \".ard-input\":\n\t\t\treturn reason(\"under .ard-input/ (loop inputs)\")\n\t\tcase \".github\":\n\t\t\treturn reason(\"under .github/\")\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\t// A file named like a reserved directory is refused too.\n\tswitch strings.ToLower(segs[0]) {\n\tcase \".ard\", \".ard-input\", \".github\":\n\t\tif len(segs) == 1 {\n\t\t\treturn reason(\"reserved name \" + segs[0])\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\tif strings.EqualFold(segs[len(segs)-1], \".gitmodules\") {\n\t\treturn reason(\"submodule configuration\")\n\t}\n\tif SecretShaped(p) {\n\t\treturn reason(\"secret-shaped file name\")\n\t}\n\treturn nil\n}\n\nvar (\n\tsecretExact = map[string]bool{\n\t\t\".env\": true, \".netrc\": true, \"_netrc\": true, \".npmrc\": true, \".pypirc\": true,\n\t\t\".htpasswd\": true, \".git-credentials\": true, \".dockercfg\": true,\n\t\t\"credentials\": true, \"credentials.json\": true,\n\t}\n\tsecretEnvOK       = map[string]bool{\".env.example\": true, \".env.sample\": true, \".env.template\": true}\n\tsecretKeyPrefixes = []string{\"id_rsa\", \"id_dsa\", \"id_ecdsa\", \"id_ed25519\"}\n\tsecretSuffixes    = []string{\".pem\", \".key\", \".p12\", \".pfx\", \".jks\", \".keystore\", \".ppk\", \".kdbx\"}\n\tsecretPairs       = []string{\".aws/credentials\", \".docker/config.json\"}\n)\n\n// SecretShaped reports whether p names a file that usually holds a credential.\nfunc SecretShaped(p string) bool {\n\tp = strings.ToLower(p)\n\tbase := path.Base(p)\n\tif secretExact[base] {\n\t\treturn true\n\t}\n\tif strings.HasPrefix(base, \".env.\") && !secretEnvOK[base] {\n\t\treturn true\n\t}\n\tfor _, pre := range secretKeyPrefixes {\n\t\tif strings.HasPrefix(base, pre) {\n\t\t\treturn true\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\tif strings.HasPrefix(base, \"service-account\") && strings.HasSuffix(base, \".json\") {\n\t\treturn true\n\t}\n\tfor _, suf := range secretSuffixes {\n\t\tif strings.HasSuffix(base, suf) {\n\t\t\treturn true\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\tfor _, pair := range secretPairs {\n\t\tif p == pair || strings.HasSuffix(p, \"/\"+pair) {\n\t\t\treturn true\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn false\n}\nbranches.go\ncode.go\ncode_test.go\ncodepath.go\ncodepath_test.go\ncommitter.go\ncommitter_test.go\nerrors.go\nguard.go\ninit.go\ninit_test.go\nmessage.go\nrefs.go\nrefs_test.go\nrequest.go\nunit_test.go\n(eval):1: no matches found: --include=*.go\n82:| `ard:needs-approval` | Merger | PR touches a protected path; merges after a human approves |\n229:The starter engineer (`.ard/triage/engineer/prompt.txt`) plans for the worker without implementing. Its inputs are `.ard-input/<issue#>/issue.md`, `comments.md` and `.ard/<issue#>/plan.md`, all untrusted. It writes the `## Implementation` section of `plan.md` with Steps, Test plan, Risk and Size, sized S, M, L or XL on a scale tied to the worker's 1,000-line guard (XL is 1,000 lines or more). It writes `triage-engineer.md` with `role: engineer` and `attempt: 1`, never a `worker` key. It answers `needs-human` for an XL change, a change to protected role and config paths or `.github/`, or a missing product or design decision, and leaves `plan.md` untouched. In Phase 1 it has no `after:`; the lead's request orders it. The committer and close-out guards confine it to `.ard/<issue#>/`, and close-out allows it only `triage-engineer.md` and the `## Implementation` section of `plan.md`; a change to `triage-lead.md`, `issue.md` or another role's section fails the job as needs-human.\n318:3. **Run the agent** with `.ard/reviewers/<role>/prompt.txt` and these untrusted inputs: `.ard-input/<issue#>/diff.patch` (the controller's diff from the merge base with `main` to the pinned head, `--stat` then the patch, taken with `--text`, `--no-textconv` and attributes read from the empty tree so a `.gitattributes` entry cannot hide a change, with this issue's `.ard/<issue#>/` left out and cut at 1 MiB), `.ard-input/<issue#>/issue.md` and `comments.md` (written by the loop, never committed), the committed `.ard/<issue#>/` (plan, triage and worker role files and logs) and the sidecar (security policy, license allowlist). The loop runs every git command against the controller clone, never in the reviewer's worktree (the agent itself may run read-only git there). In round 2 and later it also gets its previous findings and must say which are resolved (review feedback loop).\n348:- the diff touches no protected path; the list comes only from `merge.protected_paths`, with no floor in code (decided, #3), so a human who empties the list removes the gate. `ard init` proposes the defaults `.github/`, `.ard/config.yaml`, the four role trees (`.ard/scanners/`, `.ard/triage/`, `.ard/workers/`, `.ard/reviewers/`), `**/CODEOWNERS`, and the agent instructions a PR could plant to steer later agents: `CLAUDE.md`, `**/CLAUDE.md`, `AGENTS.md`, `**/AGENTS.md`, `.claude/` and `.mcp.json` (13 entries; `**/X` already matches a root `X`, the bare entries are kept for clarity). An entry ending in `/` is a directory prefix; any other entry is a doublestar glob. Matching is case-sensitive on the repo-relative path. A prefix matches on a directory boundary (`.github/` matches `.github` and `.github/x`, never `.githubx`). A glob follows doublestar: `*` stays within one path segment and `**` crosses them, so a bare `CODEOWNERS` would match only the root file. Quote globs that start with `*` in YAML. The merger checks both names of a renamed file; a malformed path counts as protected. A PR that touches one gets `ard:needs-approval` and merges on the next poll after a human approves\n384:  protected_paths: [.github/, .ard/config.yaml, .ard/scanners/, .ard/triage/, .ard/workers/, .ard/reviewers/, \"**/CODEOWNERS\", CLAUDE.md, \"**/CLAUDE.md\", AGENTS.md, \"**/AGENTS.md\", .claude/, .mcp.json]\n404:**`ard init` proposes the starter `.ard/` as a PR (decided, #27).** It builds `config.yaml` and the starter roles in memory (today `scanners/cve`, `triage/lead`, `triage/engineer`, `workers/engineer` and `reviewers/eng-review`; `intake: {mode: label}`, the protected-path defaults above, and the cve, lead, engineer, worker and eng-review overrides), compares them with `main`, commits them on a new branch `ard-init` cut from `main` through the effect layer and a narrow committer lane, pushes it and opens a PR. It writes nothing to the local working tree or to `main`, and `scan` is committed `100755`. Files that already exist on `main` with different contents are a conflict naming the paths; `--force` proposes overwriting them, and init never deletes anything. It refuses while `ard-init` exists on the remote, whatever its PR's state, so it never force-pushes over or stacks commits on a human's edits: merge or close the PR, delete the branch and rerun. If `main` already has every starter file it prints `nothing to propose`. It syncs the label catalogue first (like `ard labels sync`), so a failure leaves nothing pushed. With `--dry-run` the commit, push and PR are logged to the ledger instead of acting. A missing repo, credentials or `main` branch exits 1 with nothing written, and `ARD_GITHUB_TOKEN` auth is refused outside `--dry-run`. The PR body carries the one step the App cannot do: copy `.github/workflows/bot-commits.yml` and set the repository variable `ARD_BOT_LOGIN` (optionally `ARD_BOT_CHECK_SINCE`). It also says to merge with **Squash and merge** or **Create a merge commit**, never a fast-forward from the command line, which would put the bot's commit on main's first-parent history. **Rebase and merge** is also safe: the scratch-repo run (#85) showed GitHub records the merging human as committer of a rebase-merged commit (the bot stays the author), and the bot-commit check keys on committer, so it does not flag it. The body still names only the two merges above.\n417:- **Planted repo config does not steer agents.** A branch can carry CLAUDE.md, AGENTS.md, `.claude/` settings, hooks, skills, agents or a `.mcp.json`. The Claude adapter's flags stop all of them loading (see Executors). Residual risk: auth isolation comes from the empty per-job config dir and HOME, not a flag (the CLI reads no Keychain entry but the one keyed to the per-job dir); a host-mode job on macOS still runs as the operator's uid, so agent Bash could call `security` against the operator's login Keychain, including Claude's own entry, which is within the host-mode risk of #84 (a Dockerfile role or a dedicated macOS user for `ard serve` closes it); the subscription token is a one-year, model-only credential, rotated by running `claude setup-token` again and restarting `ard serve`; `--max-budget-usd` and `total_cost_usd` are estimates on a subscription; part of the block (AGENTS.md, and CLAUDE.md under `--restricted`) is observed behaviour that holds only on the tested CLI version and is re-checked by the integration tests; built-in plugins and the host's managed settings still apply; system reminders